<p class="gna-lede">TMJ arthrocentesis is a minimally invasive procedure in which the jaw joint is flushed with sterile fluid through one or two fine needles placed just in front of the ear. It is usually done as a day-care procedure under local anaesthetic, often with sedation, and is mainly used for a locked jaw or a painful, inflamed joint that has not settled with simpler care. Many people gain better mouth opening and less pain afterwards, but results vary, and it does not move a displaced disc back into place.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-arthrocentesis">What is TMJ arthrocentesis?</h2>
<p>The temporomandibular joint (TMJ) connects the lower jaw to the skull. Like other joints, it contains a small amount of lubricating fluid and a cushioning disc. When the joint is inflamed or the disc is stuck, that fluid can become thick with inflammatory substances, and the disc may cling to the surface above it — a bit like two wet panes of glass that will not slide apart.</p>
<p>Arthrocentesis means "joint puncture". In the jaw, the surgeon places fine needles into the upper part of the joint space and washes it through with a measured volume of sterile saline or Ringer's lactate solution. This is thought to help in several ways:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Flushing out</strong> pain-causing inflammatory substances from the joint fluid</li>
  <li><strong>Breaking the suction</strong> that can hold the disc against the socket</li>
  <li><strong>Loosening mild adhesions</strong> (thin bands of scar tissue) through gentle fluid pressure</li>
  <li><strong>Restoring smoother movement</strong> so that the jaw can open further</li>
</ul>
<p>It is sometimes called a "joint lavage" or simply a wash-out. Because nothing is cut and no camera is used, it is the least invasive of the procedures performed directly on the jaw joint.</p>
<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Arthrocentesis washes the jaw joint with sterile fluid through fine needles; there is no incision.</li>
    <li>It is used mainly for closed lock and for joint pain with inflammation that has not responded to conservative care.</li>
    <li>It is usually a day-care procedure under local anaesthetic, with or without sedation.</li>
    <li>Most people return to desk work within a day or two and follow a soft diet for about a week.</li>
    <li>Research generally supports its use, but studies are small and results vary between individuals.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="who-is-it-for">Who is arthrocentesis for?</h2>
<p>Arthrocentesis sits between conservative care (splints, physiotherapy, medicines) and surgery. A specialist may suggest it if you have:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>A closed lock</strong> — the disc has slipped forward and stays there, limiting opening — that has not improved with manipulation and conservative treatment. Our guide on <a href="/blogs/jaw-locked-closed-lock-tmj/">what a closed lock is and how it is managed</a> explains this condition in detail.</li>
  <li><strong>Persistent joint pain</strong> with signs of inflammation in the joint lining, despite a well-fitted splint and several weeks to months of good self-care.</li>
  <li><strong>Early degenerative (wear-and-tear) changes</strong> in the joint causing pain and stiffness.</li>
  <li><strong>A flare-up</strong> of joint pain and limited opening in someone with a known joint problem.</li>
</ul>
<p>It is usually <em>not</em> the right step when pain comes mainly from the chewing muscles rather than the joint itself, when the jaw clicks but works comfortably, or when the joint is fused (ankylosis) or severely damaged. In these situations other treatments are more appropriate — see our overview of <a href="/tmj-treatment">TMJ treatment options</a>.</p>

<h2 id="how-it-is-done">How the procedure is done</h2>
<h3>Before the procedure</h3>
<p>At consultation, your surgeon examines your jaw, measures your mouth opening and reviews any scans. An MRI is often used to confirm the position of the disc and the degree of inflammation. Tell your team about any blood-thinning medicines, bleeding problems, diabetes, allergies or pregnancy. You will receive written information on the procedure, its expected benefits and its risks before you sign a consent form.</p>
<h3>On the day</h3>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Anaesthesia.</strong> The skin and joint area in front of the ear are numbed with local anaesthetic. Many people also receive sedation through a drip so that they feel relaxed and drowsy. Some surgeons prefer general anaesthesia, particularly if both joints are treated.</li>
  <li><strong>Marking.</strong> The surgeon marks the entry points on a line running from the ear towards the outer corner of the eye, using bony landmarks to locate the joint.</li>
  <li><strong>Placing the needles.</strong> In the common two-needle technique, one needle lets fluid in and the second lets it flow out. A single-puncture technique, using a specially designed needle, is also used; studies suggest both work similarly.</li>
  <li><strong>Washing the joint.</strong> Fluid is flushed through the joint while the jaw is gently moved, which helps free the disc. The volume used varies between protocols.</li>
  <li><strong>Optional injection.</strong> At the end, some surgeons inject a medicine such as hyaluronic acid or a corticosteroid into the joint.</li>
</ol>
<p>The procedure itself typically takes around 20 to 40 minutes. You are monitored for a short time afterwards and usually go home the same day. If you have had sedation, you will need someone to accompany you home.</p>

