<h2 id="comparison">Splint vs arthrocentesis vs arthroscopy</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Splint and physiotherapy</th><th>Arthrocentesis</th><th>Arthroscopy</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>What it involves</td><td>Night-time acrylic appliance, exercises, self-care</td><td>Needle wash-out of the joint</td><td>Keyhole camera examination and treatment of the joint</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Anaesthesia</td><td>None</td><td>Local, often with sedation</td><td>General</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Setting</td><td>Clinic</td><td>Day-care, hospital</td><td>Day-care or overnight, hospital</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Can the surgeon see inside?</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Typical return to desk work</td><td>No time off</td><td>About 1–2 days</td><td>About 3–7 days</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Usually considered for</td><td>Almost everyone, first</td><td>Closed lock, inflamed joint not settling</td><td>Persistent joint problems, or when arthrocentesis has not helped</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>If arthrocentesis does not help enough, <a href="/tmj-arthroscopy">TMJ arthroscopy</a> is often the next option, because it allows the surgeon to see and treat adhesions or inflamed tissue directly.</p>

<h2 id="evidence">Does it work? What the evidence says</h2>
<p>Arthrocentesis has been studied for several decades. Overall, the research is encouraging but not conclusive, and it is fair to say so plainly:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Systematic reviews report improvements in mouth opening in most groups studied, and reduced pain in many.</li>
  <li>In people with a closed lock, a meta-analysis of five studies found greater improvement in opening and pain with arthrocentesis than with splints alone — but the number of studies was small.</li>
  <li>The two-needle and single-puncture techniques appear similarly effective, and the exact type or volume of fluid does not seem to change results much.</li>
  <li>Whether adding an injection (hyaluronic acid, steroid, platelet-rich plasma and others) improves results is <strong>uncertain</strong>. Some analyses suggest a benefit for certain combinations; others found no clear advantage over the wash-out alone.</li>
</ul>
<p>Many studies are small, use different methods and follow patients for limited periods. In practice, this means arthrocentesis is a reasonable step for carefully selected patients, combined with ongoing conservative care, rather than a stand-alone answer. Some people need a repeat procedure; a few go on to need arthroscopy.</p>

<h2 id="recovery">Recovery after arthrocentesis</h2>
<p>Recovery is usually quick, though it varies:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>First few hours:</strong> numbness in front of the ear and sometimes temporary weakness of the eyelid or forehead on that side as the local anaesthetic wears off. Protect the eye if it does not close fully until this passes.</li>
  <li><strong>First one to three days:</strong> swelling, an ache in the joint and a bite that feels slightly uneven on the treated side, from fluid in the joint. Cold packs, prescribed painkillers and rest help.</li>
  <li><strong>First week:</strong> a soft diet — idli, upma, khichdi, curd rice, soft dal-rice, eggs and well-cooked vegetables — and no chewing gum or hard foods. Most people return to desk work within one or two days.</li>
  <li><strong>Weeks one to six:</strong> regular jaw exercises as taught by your surgeon or physiotherapist, and your night splint if you have one. Reviews are commonly arranged at about one week and four to six weeks.</li>
</ul>
<p>Improvement often continues over several weeks. The recovery after arthroscopy is longer and is described in our guide to <a href="/blogs/tmj-arthroscopy-recovery-what-to-expect/">TMJ arthroscopy recovery week by week</a>.</p>

<h2 id="risks">Risks and side effects</h2>
<p>Arthrocentesis is considered a low-risk procedure, but it is not free of risk. Common, usually short-lived effects include swelling in front of the ear, discomfort at the needle sites, bruising, a temporary change in bite and temporary facial weakness from the local anaesthetic. Less common risks include:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Infection of the skin or the joint</li>
  <li>Bleeding from small blood vessels near the joint</li>
  <li>Fluid spreading into the surrounding tissues, causing extra swelling for a day or two</li>
  <li>Injury to the ear canal or, rarely, effects on hearing</li>
  <li>Facial nerve weakness lasting longer than the anaesthetic — uncommon and usually recovers</li>
  <li>Little or no improvement in symptoms</li>
</ul>
<div class="gna-callout gna-callout--warn">
  <p><strong>Contact your surgical team promptly</strong> if you develop fever, swelling that increases after the second day, redness or discharge at the needle sites, bleeding that does not stop with pressure, facial weakness lasting beyond the day of the procedure, ear pain with discharge or hearing loss, or pain that your medicines do not control. If you have difficulty breathing or swallowing, call 108 or go to the nearest emergency department.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="when-to-see-specialist">When to see a specialist</h2>
<p>Consider an assessment with a maxillofacial surgeon if your jaw has locked and is not releasing, if your mouth opening has reduced over weeks, or if jaw-joint pain persists despite a few months of splint therapy and self-care. Most jaw-joint problems do not need any procedure, and an examination will help clarify whether conservative care, arthrocentesis or another option suits your situation. Our guide to <a href="/blogs/tmj-splint-vs-surgery/">choosing between a splint and surgery</a> is a helpful place to start.</p>
<p>Consultations take place at our clinic in Gachibowli, Hyderabad, and procedures are carried out at NABH-accredited partner hospitals. Cashless insurance may be available, subject to your policy terms and pre-authorisation.</p>
